<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/energy-systems-and-the-grid">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An electricity grid is <strong>the largest machine ever built</strong> — a continent-spanning
    circuit balanced in real time. The energy transition is its first full redesign since
    electrification: replacing fuel with weather, and the operational challenge shifts from
    logistics of fuel to the engineering of flexibility.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the system, the trilemma, the cost revolution, the hard parts" widget="equationStep" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>System knowledge that travels</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Baseload is a concept under renovation:</strong> the old stack (big thermal plants always on) gives way to "flexibility first" — renewables + storage + demand response + interconnection. Grid operators (the people who keep the lights on) publish the cleanest data on how this is actually going.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrification multiplies grid demand:</strong> EVs, heat pumps, and industry conversion mean grids must roughly double clean output — the transition is bigger than swapping existing supply (the business lesson\'s unit economics, gigawatt-hours edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Blackouts teach system design:</strong> Texas 2021 (weatherised for neither heat nor cold), the 2003 Northeast cascade (a tree + a bug + no situational awareness) — failures reveal that reliability is a designed property, maintained continuously.</li>
    <li><strong>The just transition question:</strong> fossil jobs and tax bases concentrate in specific regions — the political-economy lesson\'s concentrated-pain pattern, with the policy response (transition funds, retraining) deciding the political feasibility of the whole project.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IRENA renewable cost data; IEA World Energy Outlook; NERC and ENTSO-E reliability reports; the thermodynamics and circuits lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
